1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,382
Tap a crystal, and a ripple of sound races through it.

2
00:00:03,382 --> 00:00:06,513
But in many materials, that ripple isn't a circle.

3
00:00:06,513 --> 00:00:11,837
Sound runs faster along the grain of wood than across it, and earthquake waves change

4
00:00:11,837 --> 00:00:15,720
speed with direction through some rocks deep inside the Earth.

5
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:21,804
So here's the question. If you measured how fast waves travel in every direction, could

6
00:00:21,804 --> 00:00:25,344
you work out exactly what the material is like inside?

7
00:00:25,344 --> 00:00:26,852
Pause and make a guess.

8
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:34,470
Start with a spring. Squash it, and it pushes back: a stiff spring hard, a soft one gently.

9
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:39,137
How hard it pushes back for each bit of squash is its stiffness.

10
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:43,402
A solid is like a grid of balls joined by springs.

11
00:00:43,402 --> 00:00:47,580
Push one end, and the squash passes from ball to ball.

12
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:50,597
That travelling squash is a sound wave.

13
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:56,255
Stiffer springs pass it on faster, and heavier balls slow it down.

14
00:00:56,255 --> 00:01:03,065
In fact, speed squared equals stiffness divided by density, so waves only reveal that

15
00:01:03,065 --> 00:01:06,831
ratio. From now on, stiffness means that ratio.

16
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:13,592
A wave can push and pull the balls along its path, or shake them sideways, in either

17
00:01:13,592 --> 00:01:16,945
of two directions. Each kind has its own speed.

18
00:01:16,945 --> 00:01:20,512
So in any one direction, you measure three speeds.

19
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:25,539
Now make the springs stiffer one way, like the grain in wood.

20
00:01:25,539 --> 00:01:30,482
A flat wave runs faster along the stiff springs than across them.

21
00:01:30,482 --> 00:01:37,174
Draw an arrow in each direction, as long as the speed there, and the tips trace a curve.

22
00:01:37,174 --> 00:01:41,204
That's called anisotropy: speed depends on direction.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,630
In three dimensions, the tips trace a surface.

24
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:49,432
Here's the fastest wave's surface for our running example: an imaginary crystal with

25
00:01:49,432 --> 00:01:52,176
no symmetry at all, from the paper's test files.

26
00:01:52,176 --> 00:01:55,320
It's lumpy, because it's stiffer some ways than others.

27
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,543
Each kind of wave has its own surface, nested inside one another.

28
00:02:00,543 --> 00:02:05,955
Here's a slice through all three. One over speed is called slowness, and it carries the

29
00:02:05,955 --> 00:02:07,012
same information.

30
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:11,012
How many numbers describe a material's stiffness?

31
00:02:11,012 --> 00:02:16,780
A tiny cube can be deformed in six basic ways: stretched along each of three directions,

32
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:19,008
or skewed in each of three planes.

33
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:25,988
Deform the cube in any of these ways, and it can push back in all six: a six by six table.

34
00:02:25,988 --> 00:02:32,356
Physics makes it a mirror image across its diagonal, so only six, plus five, plus four,

35
00:02:32,356 --> 00:02:35,869
plus three, plus two, plus one numbers are free.

36
00:02:35,869 --> 00:02:39,968
Twenty-one unknowns, like twenty-one dials you can turn.

37
00:02:40,767 --> 00:02:46,118
Now the measurements. Straight up, our crystal's three speeds are one point one two,

38
00:02:46,118 --> 00:02:48,858
one point four two, and one point six nine.

39
00:02:48,858 --> 00:02:54,273
Square them, and take three summaries: the sum, the sum of products in pairs, and the

40
00:02:54,273 --> 00:02:54,783
product.

41
00:02:55,167 --> 00:03:00,911
Knowing the three summaries is the same as knowing the three numbers, just as two numbers

42
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:04,719
that add to five and multiply to six must be two and three.

43
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:09,198
The summaries also change with direction in a tidy way.

44
00:03:09,198 --> 00:03:15,605
Write the direction as x, y and z. The sum is always a formula like this, fixed by six

45
00:03:15,605 --> 00:03:22,013
numbers. Put in straight up, so x and y are zero and z is one, and out comes six point

46
00:03:22,013 --> 00:03:23,652
one, just as measured.

47
00:03:24,033 --> 00:03:29,344
The sum of pair products follows a formula fixed by fifteen numbers, and the product

48
00:03:29,344 --> 00:03:34,718
needs twenty-eight. That's forty-nine numbers in all, every one set by the twenty-one

49
00:03:34,718 --> 00:03:35,793
stiffness values.

50
00:03:36,566 --> 00:03:39,914
Forty-nine measurements, twenty-one unknowns.

51
00:03:39,914 --> 00:03:42,889
Surely that settles it? Not necessarily.

52
00:03:42,889 --> 00:03:49,286
x squared equals four is one equation in one unknown, and it has two answers: two, and

53
00:03:49,286 --> 00:03:50,030
minus two.

54
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:56,007
Materials can do this too. These two tables describe different materials with a neat,

55
00:03:56,007 --> 00:03:58,910
box-like symmetry, differing in two entries.

56
00:03:58,910 --> 00:04:02,274
Yet in every direction, their speeds are identical.

57
00:04:02,274 --> 00:04:03,264
They are twins.

58
00:04:03,633 --> 00:04:09,807
Four mathematicians, de Hoop, Ilmavirta, Lassas and Várilly-Alvarado, proved that in

59
00:04:09,807 --> 00:04:13,262
two dimensions, a typical material has no twin.

60
00:04:13,262 --> 00:04:19,583
In three dimensions, they left it as Conjecture E: a typical material, with no special

61
00:04:19,583 --> 00:04:22,449
symmetry, is pinned down by its speeds.

62
00:04:23,233 --> 00:04:28,900
This paper proves Conjecture E. A typical three-dimensional material has no twin, not

63
00:04:28,900 --> 00:04:34,700
even a physically impossible one. And twenty-two numbers are enough: the six of the sum

64
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:40,033
formula, the fifteen of the pair formula, and the product in just one direction.

65
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:45,883
Typical means this. The materials that might have twins form a thinner set, with fewer

66
00:04:45,883 --> 00:04:48,798
dials, like a curve drawn on a sheet of paper.

67
00:04:48,798 --> 00:04:53,297
Throw a dart at the paper, and you miss the curve with probability one.

68
00:04:54,066 --> 00:04:59,737
Here's the proof idea. Suppose two tables, A and B, give the same measurements.

69
00:04:59,737 --> 00:05:05,982
Describe them by their midpoint, M, and half-gap, D, so A is M plus D, and B is M minus

70
00:05:05,982 --> 00:05:09,930
D. That's twenty-one dials for M, and twenty-one for D.

71
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:13,179
Test one: the sum formulas must match.

72
00:05:13,179 --> 00:05:19,468
That fixes six of D's numbers in terms of the others, leaving D with fifteen dials.

73
00:05:19,866 --> 00:05:22,754
Test two: the pair formulas must match.

74
00:05:22,754 --> 00:05:29,046
Once D is chosen, that's fifteen simultaneous equations for M, the straight-line kind

75
00:05:29,046 --> 00:05:35,413
from school. If all fifteen are genuinely different, they use up fifteen of M's dials,

76
00:05:35,413 --> 00:05:39,114
leaving six. So far, fifteen plus six: twenty-one.

77
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:42,739
Test three: the product in one direction must match.

78
00:05:42,739 --> 00:05:47,036
One more equation takes us to twenty, unless it's automatically true.

79
00:05:47,036 --> 00:05:49,964
The paper rules that out with an exact example.

80
00:05:50,333 --> 00:05:55,179
Take M as zero, and D a table that treats every direction the same.

81
00:05:55,179 --> 00:05:59,447
Straight up, D's numbers are minus one, minus one, and two.

82
00:05:59,447 --> 00:06:02,196
Minus D's are one, one, and minus two.

83
00:06:02,196 --> 00:06:05,379
The sums match, and so do the pair products.

84
00:06:05,379 --> 00:06:08,489
But the products, two and minus two, don't.

85
00:06:08,489 --> 00:06:11,093
So test three really removes a dial.

86
00:06:11,866 --> 00:06:17,994
But there's a catch. Some equations might secretly repeat others, like x plus y equals

87
00:06:17,994 --> 00:06:20,701
two, and two x plus two y equals four.

88
00:06:20,701 --> 00:06:26,757
That's really one equation. If only r of the fifteen are genuinely different, M keeps

89
00:06:26,757 --> 00:06:30,034
twenty-one minus r dials. More room for twins.

90
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:33,488
The heart of the paper is a balancing act.

91
00:06:33,488 --> 00:06:39,599
It proves that half-gaps with only r genuine equations are rare: they have at most r

92
00:06:39,599 --> 00:06:44,545
minus one dials. Whenever M gains freedom, D loses at least as much.

93
00:06:44,933 --> 00:06:48,763
Add them up: r minus one, plus twenty-one minus r.

94
00:06:48,763 --> 00:06:52,133
The r's cancel, and you're left with twenty.

95
00:06:52,533 --> 00:06:55,881
Here it is for every r, from fifteen down to six.

96
00:06:55,881 --> 00:06:59,366
Every bar stops at twenty, one short of twenty-one.

97
00:06:59,366 --> 00:07:04,149
And apart from zero, no half-gap has fewer than six genuine equations.

98
00:07:04,533 --> 00:07:10,070
So every possible twin pair fits in a family of at most twenty dials, while materials

99
00:07:10,070 --> 00:07:15,282
have twenty-one. Like the curve on the paper, it's too thin to cover everything.

100
00:07:15,282 --> 00:07:17,301
A typical material has no twin.

101
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:21,616
Proving 'at most r minus one' is where the computer comes in.

102
00:07:21,616 --> 00:07:26,574
A symmetry argument about rotations shows that every family of bad half-gaps must pass

103
00:07:26,574 --> 00:07:30,782
through one of two special tables, with six and eleven genuine equations.

104
00:07:30,782 --> 00:07:35,164
So the computer only has to look closely there, and check a few flat slices.

105
00:07:35,533 --> 00:07:41,178
All this uses exact whole-number arithmetic, with no rounding, partly on a clock with

106
00:07:41,178 --> 00:07:46,823
thirty-two thousand and three hours. The paper explains why a miss on that clock is a

107
00:07:46,823 --> 00:07:52,867
miss for ordinary numbers too. The results are certificates: identities anyone can multiply

108
00:07:52,867 --> 00:07:53,797
out and check.

109
00:07:54,566 --> 00:07:57,633
Real experiments measure only a few directions.

110
00:07:57,633 --> 00:08:03,114
Seven or fewer can never work. The sums give at most six useful numbers, since their

111
00:08:03,114 --> 00:08:07,552
formula has six, and the other two summaries give two per direction.

112
00:08:07,552 --> 00:08:10,814
Six, plus seven, plus seven, is twenty: one short.

113
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:14,761
Eleven random directions are enough, with probability one.

114
00:08:14,761 --> 00:08:20,104
A material plus eleven directions has forty-three dials, and every way of having a twin

115
00:08:20,104 --> 00:08:25,324
fits in forty-two. The paper also gives fifteen specific directions that work for any

116
00:08:25,324 --> 00:08:26,368
typical material.

117
00:08:26,766 --> 00:08:32,814
What about eight, nine or ten? Counting doesn't rule them out, and nobody knows yet.

118
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,538
So what's proved? A typical material has no twin.

119
00:08:36,538 --> 00:08:41,274
The proof is computer-assisted: written reasoning joined to exact certificates.

120
00:08:41,274 --> 00:08:46,311
Lean, a proof-checking program, has checked eleven key identities, but not the whole

121
00:08:46,311 --> 00:08:50,448
argument. It's been reviewed internally, not yet by outside referees.

122
00:08:50,833 --> 00:08:53,443
And what isn't? Unique doesn't mean stable.

123
00:08:53,443 --> 00:08:58,664
In one simulated test, noise of one part in a thousand in the speeds left the best fit

124
00:08:58,664 --> 00:09:02,003
to the twenty-two numbers about forty-five percent off.

125
00:09:02,003 --> 00:09:07,345
Symmetric materials can still have twins, and density is never separated from stiffness.

126
00:09:08,133 --> 00:09:13,858
To recap. One: stiffness takes twenty-one numbers, and counting measurements can't rule

127
00:09:13,858 --> 00:09:19,452
out twins. Two: split a twin pair into midpoint and half-gap, and all such pairs fill

128
00:09:19,452 --> 00:09:22,940
at most twenty dials, so a typical material has none.

129
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:27,876
Three: twenty-two summary numbers, or eleven random directions, are enough.

130
00:09:27,876 --> 00:09:28,797
Seven are not.

131
00:09:29,166 --> 00:09:34,788
Still unknown: whether eight, nine or ten directions are enough, exactly which special

132
00:09:34,788 --> 00:09:40,278
materials have twins, and how to recover stiffness reliably from noisy measurements.
